ns.\n2. Literature Review\nSupply chain resilience depends on how organisations anticipate disruption and coordinate\ntheir partners. Managers who share demand information early reduce the bullwhip effect and\nprotect service levels. This section explains why integrated planning matters and how it\nimproves performance across regions.\nKey drivers identified in the literature include:\n Information sharing\n Supplier collaboration\n Inventory visibility\n Demand forecasting accuracy\nSupply chain resilience depends on how organisations anticipate disruption and coordinate\ntheir partners. Managers who share demand information early reduce the bullwhip effect and\nprotect service levels. This section explains why integrated planning matters and how it\nimproves performance across regions.\n Firms that integrate planning with suppliers achieve markedly lower inventory costs and\nfewer stockouts over time.\n Collaborative forecasting allows partners to respond to demand shifts before they create\ncostly disruptions downstream.\n3. Analysis of the Case\nTable 1: Supplier Scores\nSupplier Score Lead time\nAlpha Ltd 82 6 weeks\nBeta Corp 74 9 weeks\nGamma Inc 91 4 weeks\nSupply chain resilience depends on how organisations anticipate disruption and coordinate\ntheir partners. Managers who share demand information early reduce the bullwhip effect and\n\nprotect service levels. This section explains why integrated planning matters and how it\nimproves performance across regions.\nTable 2: Risk Register\nRisk Mitigation\nPort congestion Diversify shipping routes\nSingle sourcing The company should qualify a second\nsupplier for every critical component because\na single disruption currently halts production\nfor several weeks.\nSupply chain resilience depends on how organisations anticipate disruption and coordinate\ntheir partners. Managers who share demand information early reduce the bullwhip effect and\nprotect service levels. This section explains why integrated p